## 5. FORECAST

Maintain two independent probability sets. They are not mutually exclusive with each other, so keep them separate rather than forcing one list to 100%.

### Track 1 — Energy and geopolitical (next 6–12 months)

Scenario A — Grinding normalization
Hormuz progressively normalizes, energy remains expensive but manageable, U.S. growth slows without a severe recession.

Scenario B — Escalation / persistent supply shock
Hormuz/Gulf disruption persists or worsens, oil remains very expensive, inflation rises, consumer demand weakens and recession risk increases.

Scenario C — Rapid settlement
A credible political/military settlement restores substantial Gulf shipping and production, oil falls sharply and inflation/recession risks decline.

Probabilities total 100%.

### Track 2 — AI capital cycle (next 6–12 months)

Scenario D — Sustained buildout
Capex guidance holds or rises, financing remains available on reasonable terms, AI investment continues contributing positively to growth.

Scenario E — Orderly moderation
Capex growth decelerates, some projects are delayed or downsized, concentration unwinds gradually. Growth contribution fades without a credit event.

Scenario F — Disorderly retrenchment
Capex is cut sharply, AI-linked credit widens or a significant borrower fails to refinance, concentrated equity positions unwind quickly, and the withdrawal of investment spending becomes a material drag on GDP.

Probabilities total 100%.

